# ⛔ 6.7.14 (D33 A, the user's decision, 2026-10-16) — `f64v2_add(&a, &b)` GOES TO THE POINTER FORM `f64v2_add_ptr` ON
# EVERY TARGET, in a declaration's initialiser too. PARSE_FNCALL routes a call whose first argument is `&x` to its
# `<base>_ptr` sibling (`_fnc_route`, v5.10.39), but Win64's own-call vector receives — `var v: f64v2 = f(..)`, `v =
# f(..)`, a wrapped or D30 call temp (`_vrcv_pe_call`), PARSE_RETURN's arm — emit the call themselves (the hidden
# result pointer) and called the value form with the addresses, which only worked because Win64's value-form
# parameter is address-passed; refusing `&x` there (D33) needs them routed as the call path routes. The cursor on the
# call `fi` (`_vcall_fi`'s): entered past its `(` as `_vcall_enter` enters it, routed first — a plain `name(&x, ..)`
# whose `<name>_ptr` returns the same vector (the hidden result pointer is the same) goes to that sibling, its checks
# run on it as PARSE_FNCALL's do; an explicit instance `g<T>(..)` is not routed (nor is it there: no instance has a
# sibling). The fn to call. CHANGELOG [6.7.14]
fn _pe_venter(S, fi): i64 {
